WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
I’m Geoff McFetridge and this Beastie Boys Story Illustrated.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:17.920
I started collaborating with the Beastie Boys around 1996 when I became the art director for Grand Royale Magazine.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.880
As a fan of the Beastie Boys and working with them on and off for 25 years

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:27.680
I think it gives me a broad perspective of this band I have so much respect for.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:34.320
In making these posters I was really interested in focusing on the Beastie Boys’ creativity in their work.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:43.480
And I feel like using the iPad was a great tool to just spontaneously create in a very quick way.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.880
The sketches I did on the iPad to begin the project was like well,

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.400
can I do one drawing that captures anything to do with the spirit of the Beastie Boys.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.760
I wanted to have them moshing in a circle.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.600
The instruments are gone, they’ve picked up the mics

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:06.280
so this is using the physicality of the guys and turning it into some sort of hieroglyphic language explaining the energy at the time

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:10.160
but then the thing I added at the end was this Mobius mic cord.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.480
It’s a reference to this style the way they recorded the music all rapping the same line

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:18.520
and bouncing back and forth but also the Beastie Boys as one entity.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:28.080
This is the check your head era. The shock of the Beastie Boys not just with microphones but with instruments.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:38.680
This poster was them at their most abstract but sort of casting this shadow that they were this outsized influence on the world,

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:47.520
making this music that was so visceral and maybe more familiar, it felt like it came out of my friend’s parents’ basement.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:53.760
They just look like skater guys, the wonky anonymity of them but they carried a lot of weight.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:02:00.960
They’re attached which is sort of a theme in all the posters—they’re one unit melted together.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:14.240
The “Sabotage” video was seismic. When this video came out it was a powerful piece of culture that came out of the Beastie Boys, but it was also Spike.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:22.720
These two groups of people at their best, they made themselves icons, they sort of disappeared into these caricatures

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:30.240
and it was primarily car, this Caprice drawn here from memory that basically becomes a cop car.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:38.080
This is the one poster that got a ton of movement which I think is funny because it’s an era of costumes and pretending and play.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.360
And I think that aspect of being a band of both serious and full of attitude,

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:51.040
but at the same time it’s the longest game of dress up ever played in front of millions of people.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.000
I sort of made it speaking about the Beastie Boys act as one unit.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:10.400
This robot costume, each one of them have a part of it, the mastermind, the tape loop maker, and Mike D with one big foot and this giant arm.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:12.640
None of it’s really functioning.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.760
You see, as I evolved this poster it went from to them standing there—what made it better

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:24.480
was if I gave them more attitude if they started thrusting their arms in the air they were sort of like, “Yes don’t **** with us.”

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:34.080
But in the meantime, they’re in these ridiculous cardboard robot costumes, but they still have this BBoy, “we’re the best, don’t step to us” vibe.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:39.840
They made something culturally that’s more real than the real them.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:46.400
Culture goes on, it lives in a way more than actual humans.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.720
There’s something really sort of important that I want to capture in this last poster,

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:04.320
that’s why I wrote the title for this before I wrote this drawing which was A Spiritual Quest on the Front Line of Popular Culture.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:15.680
Mike D is like the struggle, he’s like coiled energy about to go.  Adam Horovitz is always in the air to out-rap, out-BBoy everybody on the block.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:25.920
And then Yauch is like on the wheel of life, like he’s much closer to the center, he’s moved into the middle and he’s just, his energy is just a spiritual energy.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:32.800
What’s sort of funny about this, but then is also poignant because he’s also the one that we’ve lost.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.920
And so the magic of it is also wrapped in a sort of sadness.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:45.440
There was so much of their artistic development that was done at the zenith of their fame.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:52.320
Each one of these images is sort of capturing the guys at like a physical iconic truth about themselves.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:58.880
I draw so much, the amount of time a drawing takes makes a real difference how much work I can get done.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:05.360
Working on the iPad I can very quickly start to sketch and come up with ideas to a finished product.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:08.800
I think the Beastie Boys Story is so relevant now.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.160
They were making stuff for each other.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:18.400
You make culture that is most relevant to the world right around you, which most often is your friends.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:24.880
Make work for your friends.  For me that’s the best of culture, that’s the stuff I love.

